const scandalArticle = {
  slug: "scandal",
  title: "She Was Meant To Be The Scandal",
  landingTitle: "She Was Meant To Be The Scandal",
  summary:
    "Forty-eight hours before the biggest promotion of her life, Ava Vale wakes beside a stranger with a dead phone, a missing fraud file, and a scandal designed to destroy her.",
  intro:
    "Ava built her career protecting powerful reputations. Then someone turned her into the story. With forty-eight hours left before the promotion that should change everything, she has to untangle a staged scandal, a missing file, and the one man who may know why she was meant to fall.",
  chapters: [
    {
      title: "Forty-Eight Hours",
      paragraphs: [
        `In forty-eight hours, Ava Vale was supposed to become the youngest partner in Vale & Howe's history. The announcement had been rehearsed, the champagne probably already ordered, the congratulations already gathering behind smiling teeth. She had spent nine years learning how powerful people survived disaster. She knew how to frame a leak, how to bury a rumor under a cleaner headline, how to look calm while someone else's life burned down in front of a camera. Her gift had always been control. That was why the first thing she felt when she opened her eyes was not pain, though her head was splitting and her mouth tasted like old metal. It was terror. The room was wrong. The sheets were dark charcoal silk. The ceiling above her was raw concrete crossed by black beams. Men's cologne hung in the air over something cleaner, colder, almost medicinal. And beside her, sprawled on his stomach with one tattooed arm flung over a white pillow, was a stranger who looked like the kind of man Ava's firm warned clients never to be photographed near.`,
        `Her dress was on the floor, torn at one strap. One heel was overturned under a low chair. On the side table sat a cut-crystal tumbler, an empty bottle of water, and the kind of visual wreckage that made her pulse stumble: a lipstick-smudged napkin, a snapped chain from her clutch, and enough suggestive chaos to tell a story before she even spoke. Ava shot upright too fast and nearly blacked out. The stranger moved at the same moment, not waking, just turning enough for the sheet to slip low across a broad back marked with ink and scars. Every tabloid instinct in her brain snapped to attention. This looked damning. It was meant to look damning. That thought arrived before memory did, and it landed harder. Because Ava remembered leaving a private client dinner at Luca's, the impossible-members-only club everyone in the city pretended they had outgrown and still used for off-record deals. She remembered Sienna laughing in the lift. She remembered a rooftop door opening on cold air. After that, the night dissolved into static.`,
        `Her phone was on the floor beneath the chair. Relief flashed hot and stupidly bright until she grabbed it and found the screen black, unresponsive, dead in a way a drained battery was not. No vibration. No startup logo. Nothing. Her handbag was missing entirely. So was the encrypted backup key she carried in the inner pocket when she needed to move client material off the office servers. That file. The one she had been too careful, too nervous, too certain to leave behind. A confidential folder that tied one of the firm's most protected clients to shell invoices, fake vendor contracts, and a payments trail so ugly it had made her hands cold while she read it. She had not stolen it. She had preserved it. Because once she saw what it meant, she had understood that someone inside the firm was helping cover it. And now the evidence was gone, her phone was dead, and she was half-dressed in a stranger's bed two days before the single most important vote of her career.`,
        `The man beside her inhaled sharply and rolled onto his back. He was even more dangerous-looking awake than asleep: dark hair, tired eyes, a face built from hard lines and the kind of composure that made panic feel naive. Recognition cracked through her headache a second later. Luca Cross. Owner of Luca's. Rumored fixer. Rumored menace. Rumored savior, depending on who was telling the story and how much they owed him. He focused on her at once, not surprised to find her sitting rigid at the edge of his bed. If anything, he looked like he had been expecting the exact angle of her fear. Then his gaze dropped to the dead phone in her hand, and his expression changed from watchful to grim. “Good,” he said, voice rough with sleep and something sharper underneath it. “You're awake.” He pushed himself up on one elbow, held her stare, and added, “You need to see what your phone sent.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Wiped Phone",
      paragraphs: [
        `Luca did not reach for her. That, absurdly, was the first thing that made Ava listen. Men who thought panic belonged to them always closed distance too quickly. Luca stayed where he was, sheet caught at his waist, one forearm resting on a bent knee as if she were a witness he could not afford to spook. He slid his own phone across the mattress instead. On the screen was a screenshot from Ava's account, time-stamped 2:11 a.m. It was an email sent to Warren Krall, the firm's biggest crisis client, cc'd to two board members and copied to a journalist whose inbox Ava would sooner have filled with acid. The message was a masterpiece of self-destruction. It implied Krall's account was unstable, that his matter had become unsafe to carry, and that Ava had retained personal copies of confidential materials in case she needed leverage. She did not have to imagine the reading of it. She knew exactly how it would sound in a courtroom, a board meeting, a press leak, a partnership vote. Disloyal. Reckless. Extortionate. Finished.`,
        `“I didn't send this,” she said, hating how obvious the words sounded. Luca nodded once, like he had already passed that hurdle and moved to the one behind it. He told her the message had gone out minutes after she disappeared from the club's private floor. One of his floor managers flagged the name because Krall's security team had been asking questions at the bar. Ava looked down at the dead slab in her palm and finally noticed the seam where the casing met the screen. Tiny pry marks. Her stomach turned. Someone had not just used her phone. They had stripped it. “It wasn't drunk texting,” Luca said. “It was a job.” He got out of bed then, pulling on black trousers from the chair with brisk efficiency, as if decency and urgency were equally practical tasks. “And whoever did it wanted you ruined before business hours.”`,
        `Ava forced herself to think like the person she had trained herself to be when every instinct screamed to collapse. Sequence. Motive. Damage. Who benefited if she was discredited by sunrise? Sienna's face flickered first, not because Ava suspected her yet, but because Sienna had known everything: the partnership timetable, Krall's volatility, the encrypted file Ava had been too stupidly proud of trusting her with in fragments. No. Not fragments. Context. Enough context to know it mattered. Ava pressed a hand to her temple. “If the phone is wiped, maybe the file is gone,” she said. The sentence came out half hope, half grief. Gone meant she had failed. Gone also meant the people behind it might stop. Luca gave her a look so flat it stripped the hope clean away. “People don't stage a scandal around a missing device because they want one file erased,” he said. “They do it because they need the person who can identify the file to sound untrustworthy when it appears somewhere else.”`,
        `That landed like a second concussion. The file was not the collateral damage. It was the reason. Ava saw the whole shape of it then, ugly and elegant at once: humiliate her, poison her client relationship, push the partnership committee into self-protection, and make any truth she told sound like the frantic excuse of a woman caught in her own mess. The sex-adjacent wreckage in the room, the timing, the message, the dead phone. Camouflage. Not for infidelity. For theft. Luca crossed to the kitchen counter and returned with a glass of water and two tablets she did not trust but swallowed anyway because she needed her head back. “Tell me exactly what was on the phone,” he said. Ava closed her eyes, saw the hidden folder icon, the file inside it, the names braided through invoices and retention notes. Then she opened them again and felt the last of her denial split open. “A confidential fraud file,” she said quietly. “And if they have it, they didn't just want me embarrassed.” She looked at him, finally willing to say the larger truth aloud. “They wanted me silenced before morning.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Missing File",
      paragraphs: [
        `Ava had spent her whole career in rooms where people called theft strategy and betrayal governance. That was why the file had frightened her so much the moment she understood it. Krall's matter was supposed to be a reputational containment exercise: some questionable vendor payments, an aggressive internal audit, a few board tensions that needed careful press language and cleaner minutes. That was what the official narrative said. But the documents Ava had copied into the encrypted folder told a worse story. Money had been routed through dead subsidiaries, backdated agreements had been used to justify payouts, and one familiar partner at the firm kept surfacing in threads he was never supposed to touch. It was not just Krall's mess. It smelled like a laundering machine built from polite signatures and executive trust. She had not yet decided whether she was preserving proof for protection or because some buried piece of her still believed exposure might matter. Now she knew the people behind it had decided for her.`,
        `Luca listened without interrupting, leaning against the kitchen island with both hands braced on the marble as if he were holding back the instinct to pace. The apartment suited him too well: severe lines, expensive restraint, nothing accidental. It irritated Ava that his control made her own feel shakier. “Who knew you had the file?” he asked. “Not the file,” Ava corrected automatically. “No one knew I had the actual file.” Then she stopped. “But Sienna knew I was worried. She knew there was something in Krall's account that didn't match the story. She knew I stayed late because I was checking document trails.” Saying Sienna's name made her chest tighten in a way pure suspicion did not deserve. Sienna was not just a colleague. Sienna was weekend brunches and whispered postmortems after board dinners, late-night cab rides, emergency mascara, the only person who had seen Ava panic before and still thought she looked composed. Betrayal could not wear that face. Could it?`,
        `Luca pushed away from the island and crossed to a cabinet near the entryway. From a drawer he took out a small black envelope and handed it to her. Inside was the card key she vaguely remembered being handed at Luca's private floor the previous night. Her name was written on the back in thick marker. Under it, one word: Rooftop. “You asked your friend to cover for you while you went upstairs,” Luca said. “That much I know. My cameras don't record the roof terrace, but the service corridor does.” Ava stared at the card until the letters blurred. She could feel memory at the edge of returning, not as pictures yet but as pressure. The cold rooftop air. The sense she had gone there for a reason. “Why were you watching?” she asked. That question had been building between them since the moment he said her email had been flagged. Luca's jaw shifted. Not anger. Calculation. “Because the same people tied to your client matter have been moving through my club for months,” he said. “And because the last time I ignored that pattern, someone I loved paid for it.”`,
        `He did not explain further, and Ava did not push because his face made it clear the story behind that line would not survive a casual question. Instead, he took his phone back, opened his photo roll, and scrolled to an image captured at 1:37 a.m. It had been taken from a hallway camera feed, grainy but unmistakable. Ava was in it, half-turned toward the lift, one hand on her clutch, looking pale and furious. Beside her stood Sienna, close enough to seem protective. Behind them, blurred by motion, was a man Ava did not recognize reaching toward them from the service corridor. The more Ava looked, the worse the image became. Because Sienna was not looking at Ava. She was looking at the clutch. At the hand holding it. And in the next frame Luca showed her, Sienna's hand was on Ava's phone. “This was right before you vanished upstairs,” Luca said. Ava lifted her gaze to his and felt the floor drop out a second time that morning. “Sienna was there,” she whispered. Luca's expression did not soften. “And in the next shot,” he said, “she leaves with your phone.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Phone In Sienna's Hand",
      paragraphs: [
        `Ava zoomed in until the image broke into grain and light. It did not help. Sienna's face was turned at the same familiar angle she used when she was thinking faster than everyone around her and pretending she was not. Her fingers wrapped around Ava's phone with a confidence that made no sense if she was simply rescuing it. The timestamp mattered too. One-thirty-seven. Sienna had texted Ava at one-forty-eight saying she had left early because she was suddenly sick. Ava knew that because Sienna had shown her the thread herself that morning once, joking that the rooftop air at Luca's probably carried enough expensive perfume to kill a horse. Memory recoiled against the contradiction. If Sienna had left at one-thirty-seven with Ava's phone, then the text at one-forty-eight was part of the same lie. Ava hated how quickly her mind began rearranging itself around the possibility. Not because suspicion was impossible. Because it fit too neatly around small moments she had once waved away: questions asked too casually, interest in the partnership vote disguised as concern, the almost invisible frustration in Sienna's face whenever someone called Ava the obvious choice.`,
        `“This doesn't prove she set you up,” Luca said, and for the first time all morning he sounded careful rather than certain. “It proves she had your phone and lied about her timeline.” Ava laughed once, a short flat sound she would have despised hearing from anyone else. “You say that like it's better.” She stood and regretted it immediately when the room tilted. Luca was beside her before she fell, one hand steadying her elbow, the other hovering at her waist without quite touching. Even that restraint irritated her. He felt too deliberate, too observant, too skilled at not forcing trust while making himself hard to ignore. Ava pulled free on reflex and crossed to the window. Morning had broken fully over the river. The city looked offensively normal. Delivery vans. Office towers. People already choosing coffees and lies. Somewhere downtown, someone had likely forwarded her false email to the exact people who would weaponize it fastest. “I need to get home,” she said. “I need clothes. I need a charger. I need to see whether the leak has gone internal yet.”`,
        `“You need five minutes without making yourself easier to track,” Luca said. He pointed toward the dead phone. “If they wiped it, they may also want to know when you try to recover it. If your friend has the physical device or passed it on, she already knows you're blind this morning. That's useful. Keep her thinking that.” Ava turned back to him slowly. It was not the advice that unsettled her. It was the fact that it was exactly the advice she would have given a client. Create uncertainty. Deny the other side the timing of your response. He saw the recognition land and did not smile. “You work in crisis,” he said. “So do I, in my own way.” He moved toward the bedroom and came back with a folded black shirt, crisp and obviously expensive. “Wear this until you can get the rest sorted.” Ava stared at it, then at him. “You have a whole emergency wardrobe for women waking up in your apartment?” she asked. “No,” Luca said. “I have sisters, staff, and bad nights in a city full of men who confuse access with permission.”`,
        `That answer should not have relieved her as much as it did. She took the shirt. It smelled faintly of cedar and laundry soap, nothing like the room. As she buttoned it with stiff fingers, Luca's phone buzzed in his hand. He looked at the screen, and the stillness in his face sharpened into something colder. “Too late,” he said. He turned the phone so she could see. A gossip account had posted a blurry corridor shot of her at Luca's, captioned with just enough poison to turn speculation into fact. A second notification followed from inside the firm. Mandatory partner meeting moved forward. Attendance required. Ava felt the first real rush of panic punch through her practiced calm. The story was no longer waiting for her to answer it. It was already alive.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Don't Go Home Yet",
      paragraphs: [
        `The worst thing about a live scandal was the speed at which everyone generous became strategic. Ava had taught that lesson to clients a hundred times. She had never expected to feel it in her own bloodstream. Within minutes there were three more notifications on Luca's phone from people who should not have had any reason to know where she had been. A junior associate she barely mentored had sent, hope you're okay, with the verbal spacing of someone pretending not to know more than she did. Her fiancé-turned-ex, Graham, had left two missed calls despite the fact they had not spoken in weeks. Someone inside the firm was feeding the story outward and inward at once. If Ava went back to her apartment immediately, she might arrive ahead of one photographer and behind six screen grabs. If she went to the office first, she would do it in borrowed clothes with no access to her own devices and a brain that still skipped when she tried to remember the roof. Every move felt like choosing which version of ruin looked most elegant.`,
        `Luca did not tell her what to do. He simply laid out what he knew. The corridor leak had not come from the public side of the club's systems, which meant someone had requested it, copied it, or photographed it from a protected feed. The private room on the roof had been booked under a shell reservation that now no longer existed in the active log. One of the men seen near Ava and Sienna had used a name Luca's staff could not verify. None of that cleared anyone. It did something worse. It confirmed design. “If you run home now,” Luca said, “you'll be reacting to a story someone else timed. If you slow down ten minutes, you might find out what they needed next.” Ava hated that he was right. She hated even more that he knew how much she hated it. She set his phone on the counter and forced herself to breathe through the humiliation. “Fine,” she said. “Ten minutes. Then I leave.”`,
        `The ten minutes became tactical because Luca made them so. He had one assistant send clothes to the building through a back entrance without using Ava's name. He had another confirm whether Vale & Howe's car service had been asked for her that morning. It had not. That meant the firm either assumed she would hide or wanted her to scramble. He poured her coffee, black and too strong, and when she took it he asked one question that mattered more than it seemed. “If the file appears today, who benefits first from saying you forged it?” Ava answered before thinking. “Martin Howe.” One of the founding partners. Charming in the exact manner men of his generation called paternal while hiding knives in it. Martin had publicly backed Ava's promotion because he always backed winners once the result looked obvious. But the file put his name too close to vendor approvals he should never have touched. If it surfaced, the easiest defense would be to say Ava had built a conspiracy theory out of misread paperwork. Easier still if she already looked unstable.`,
        `A knock sounded at the door. Not the assistant. Too early. Luca's face changed again, that quiet deadly focus she was beginning to recognize as his version of alarm. He motioned for silence and checked the screen beside the door. No camera feed showed the hallway. “Service override,” he said under his breath. Whoever was outside had managed to darken the corridor lens. Ava's fingers tightened around the mug until hot coffee lapped over her knuckles. Luca moved her behind the kitchen wall with a touch at her elbow so brief it barely counted as contact, then opened a narrow drawer and took out nothing theatrical, just a slim steel flashlight and his own dead calm. The knock came again, slower this time, almost polite. Then a woman's voice floated through the wood. Soft. Familiar. Perfectly pitched to sound worried. “Ava?” Sienna called. “I know you're in there. Please open the door.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "First Memory Flash",
      paragraphs: [
        `Sienna's voice at the door hit Ava like a scent from childhood: immediate, irrational, impossible to separate from trust. Her body leaned toward it before her mind caught up. Luca saw the movement and gave the smallest shake of his head. That should have been enough to harden her. Instead it opened a crack in memory. The rooftop. Not the room, not the bed. The roof. Wind lifting the edge of her coat. Sienna too close, talking too quickly. A line Ava had not understood at the time because she had still believed the night was only tense, not dangerous: you are making this harder than it has to be. Then a different voice behind them. Male. Mild. Asking whether Ava wanted another drink. After that came a flash of Luca's hand around her wrist, not rough, just decisive, steering her away from the service stair as if he had recognized something she had not. The memory vanished as quickly as it came, leaving her heart racing harder than before.`,
        `“Don't answer,” Luca said quietly. Sienna called again, layering fear into the second attempt with enough skill to make Ava wonder whether it had always been there. “I have your phone,” she said through the door. “Ava, please. I can explain.” Ava closed her eyes. That line should have resolved something. Instead it made everything blurrier. Why bring the phone here if Sienna wanted the device hidden? Why come herself when she could send another message or another person? The answer rose cold and obvious. Because she needed to see what Ava remembered. Because a ruined woman was useful, but a ruined woman with surviving evidence was a risk. Luca stepped closer without crowding her. “If she has the phone, she wants more than forgiveness,” he said. “She wants information.” Ava nodded, because there was nothing left to protect inside denial. But when Sienna's voice cracked on the third plea, grief cut through suspicion so sharply Ava had to bite the inside of her cheek to stop herself from calling back.`,
        `The knock stopped. Silence pooled. Ava counted to ten, then twenty. Nothing. Luca checked the dead hallway feed again. “She's gone,” he said. Ava moved to the door anyway and pressed her palm against the wood, stunned by the childish urge to open it a moment too late. “She knows I'm here,” she said. “Maybe. Or she guessed,” Luca replied. “Either way, she wants you reacting emotionally.” Ava laughed again, this time softer. “That's usually my line.” He glanced at her, and for a moment the tension between them shifted into something that would have been almost intimate under any other circumstances. Not softness. Recognition. Two people who knew manipulation when they saw it and were exhausted enough to admit the cost. Ava looked away first. On the counter, his phone lit again with a new alert from a gossip site that had upgraded the corridor leak into a suggestive headline with her full name spelled correctly. Someone powerful was feeding them details. Someone close.`,
        `Another memory shard struck while she stared at the screen: her own hand shoving the encrypted folder deeper into her clutch, her voice telling Sienna, if anything happens, it never goes to Martin. She had said it. She remembered saying it. Sienna's face in that moment had not looked frightened. It had looked resigned. Like someone hearing a door close on a future she wanted. Ava turned so fast she nearly dropped the coffee cup. “I didn't get drunk and confess everything,” she said. “I knew enough to hide the file. I knew enough to refuse someone.” Luca's eyes lifted sharply to hers. “Good,” he said. “Then we're not chasing what you lost. We're chasing the moment they failed to take it cleanly.” He was already reaching for his jacket when Ava asked the question she suddenly dreaded most. “And if they didn't get the file last night?” Luca paused at the doorway to the bedroom. “Then the person who still has it,” he said, “is the one they're about to squeeze next.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Damage Control",
      paragraphs: [
        `By the time Ava stepped into Vale & Howe's offices in borrowed black silk and restored lipstick, the building had already decided what kind of woman she was today. Reception staff who normally straightened when she crossed the lobby found urgent reasons to study their monitors. One partner from litigation offered her a smile so careful it felt like a legal document. The firm's internal Slack channels would be pretending professionalism while privately dining on her. Ava understood every layer because she had designed those survival habits herself. She had told people which rumors to acknowledge, which to outrun, which to starve. She had never accounted for the humiliation of walking into her own blueprint. Sienna was waiting outside the glass conference room with red-rimmed eyes and the perfect coat, looking like a woman who had spent the morning trying not to cry and still remembered to steam her sleeves. She crossed to Ava immediately. “Thank God,” she whispered, wrapping both hands around Ava's forearms. “I have been trying to protect you from all of this.”`,
        `Ava let the contact happen because refusal would show too much too soon. Up close, Sienna smelled like her usual bergamot perfume and something else underneath it, sharper, metallic, maybe anxiety or simply Ava's new distrust rewriting familiar details. “Protect me how?” Ava asked lightly. Sienna's gaze flickered toward the conference room. “By keeping Martin from saying anything irreversible before you could explain.” It was a good answer. Almost too good. Ava tilted her head as if grateful, while memorizing every syllable. “And where were you last night?” she asked. Sienna did not freeze. That was the worst part. She sighed and delivered the story she had clearly rehearsed: she felt sick, left early, lost sight of Ava after the roof access hall, assumed Ava had chosen to stay, and woke to the leak like everyone else. “I came to your apartment, but you weren't there,” she added softly. “Then someone told me they had seen Luca Cross's car near the river building.” Not how. Not who. Just enough plausibility to sound organic. Ava's chest tightened. If Sienna had guessed that building, she was either brilliant or involved.`,
        `The partner meeting was a masterpiece of institutional cowardice. Martin Howe opened with sympathy so over-polished it squeaked. The firm did not comment on personal matters, he said, but concerns had arisen about confidentiality, judgment, and client trust. Ava almost admired the choreography. No one explicitly accused her of sleeping with the wrong man. They did not need to. The leaked email and corridor photos had already done the reputational labor. She answered with the only weapon still available to her: precision. She denied sending the message. She stated that her phone had been compromised. She requested digital forensics on every account access linked to her credentials between midnight and three a.m. She asked whether anyone present had authorized release of security images connected to a private client venue. That last question disturbed the room more than the first three combined. Martin's face did not change, but his pen stilled. One of the general counsel deputies looked down too quickly. Ava felt it: resistance, not concern. The scandal had not merely arrived here. It had been welcomed.`,
        `After the meeting, Sienna fell into step beside her as they walked toward Ava's office. “You did well,” she murmured, voice warm with exactly the right amount of admiration. “If we move fast, we can still control the narrative.” We. The word scraped. Ava unlocked her office and immediately saw the second violation: drawers half an inch out of line, the framed photo on her credenza angled wrong, the backup notebook missing from the place beneath her keyboard tray where she had kept it for years. Sienna saw her stop and moved closer. “What is it?” she asked. Ava did not answer. She was looking at the one thing left on the desk that should not have been there at all: a glossy black membership token from Luca's club, placed dead center where she would have to notice it. On the back, scratched into the metal, were four words in a hand she did not recognize. Ask your friend again.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Luca's Real Reason",
      paragraphs: [
        `Luca met her two hours later in the service lane behind a florist three blocks from the firm, which was not a detail Ava liked but was exactly the kind of detail she now trusted. He was leaning against a dark motorbike helmet tucked under one arm, looking out of place in daylight and somehow even less trustworthy because of it. Ava handed him the membership token without greeting. His mouth tightened the instant he read the scratched message. “That's not from me,” he said. “I assumed that when it was delivered theatrically,” Ava replied. She told him about the office search, the meeting, the forensics request that had suddenly made Martin Howe interested in adjournment, and the way Sienna's concern kept landing just a fraction too perfectly. Luca listened with the same unnerving concentration he gave everything, as if he sorted emotion and evidence in the same internal cabinet and reached for whichever one mattered first. “Then they know you still haven't collapsed,” he said. “Which means the first wave didn't finish the job.”`,
        `Ava folded her arms, partly against the wind and partly because Luca's calm kept pulling confession out of her too easily. “You still haven't explained why you were watching any of this in the first place.” He looked past her for a moment, at the delivery vans edging through traffic and the office workers pretending not to hear themselves laugh. When he spoke, the stripped-down force in his voice made the lane feel smaller. “Because three years ago my sister walked into an internal review with evidence against men who were supposed to protect her,” he said. “And within a week she was unstable, disloyal, and unemployable on paper.” Ava said nothing. He continued anyway, perhaps because once that gate opened it demanded the whole sentence. His sister, Mara, had worked in a financial compliance team tied to luxury development projects, the same ecosystem where Krall's companies surfaced repeatedly. She found documents she should not have found. She trusted the wrong superior. She ended up framed as the source of the irregularity instead of its witness. “She didn't disappear,” Luca said. “But she lost everything that made her life feel like hers. And the people who did it kept getting invited to expensive rooms.”`,
        `That was why he had been watching Luca's private floor: not because he trafficked in scandal, but because the same names, the same intermediaries, the same careful half-criminal polish kept circulating through it. He had built a quiet map of who met whom, who arrived separately and left together, who favored cash, who used shell bookings, who pulled security favors as if they were birthrights. Ava felt something inside her soften against her will. Not trust. Understanding. It was worse, because understanding made him harder to dismiss. “You thought I was one of them,” she said. Luca held her gaze. “I thought you might be adjacent,” he answered. “Then I heard you on the roof.” Her pulse jumped. “Heard what?” Luca exhaled slowly, like he had hoped not to give her that memory before she found it herself. “You were fighting to keep your bag. You said if Martin saw the file before the board did, people would disappear it.” Ava's knees nearly failed. She had said it. Out loud. On the roof. To Sienna? To whoever else was there?`,
        `Luca stepped closer, not touching her, just anchoring the moment before it tipped into panic. “You weren't reckless,” he said. “You were cornered.” The lane noise seemed to pull away from her for a second. “And you took me home because?” Ava asked. His jaw moved. “Because by the time I got you off that roof, one of them was already calling a photographer.” The answer hit with humiliating force. He had not found her at the end of a wild night. He had interrupted the machinery of a setup. Ava looked away toward the street because if she kept looking at him, she might have to let gratitude in before she was ready. Her phone buzzed then — the temporary burner he had insisted she carry until they knew more. Unknown number. One new message. A photo of her own wiped phone sitting on a white café table beside a latte and Sienna's hand. Under it, seven words. Come alone if you want it back.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Sister",
      paragraphs: [
        `Ava did not answer Sienna's message immediately. That restraint felt like dragging herself across glass, but she had learned enough in twenty-four hours to distrust urgency whenever it arrived dressed as choice. Instead she made Luca tell her the rest of Mara's story. Not because she needed convincing, but because stories showed where people went soft, where they lied, where they were still trapped inside the version of themselves that failed someone once and had not forgiven it. Luca took her to a quiet table in the back room of a bakery that looked too domestic for his face and therefore became the first place all day Ava could breathe. Mara had been younger than Ava when it happened, brilliant and meticulous, the kind of woman older men praised in public and panicked over in private because she remembered every document they wished she'd forget. When she tried to report the pattern she found, her superior suggested rest. Then came a leak about “emotional instability.” Then missing files. Then the professional quarantine that polite industries preferred because it left no fingerprints. “By the time the truth surfaced,” Luca said, “she didn't have enough left to want vindication.”`,
        `Ava wrapped both hands around a cup of tea she did not remember ordering. The story should have felt adjacent. Instead it pressed too close. She thought of all the times she had watched women vanish from high-level conversations for reasons that sounded procedural and felt personal. She thought of Martin Howe saying let's be careful about perception whenever a female client got angry in a room. She thought of Sienna, so clever about the politics of survival, deciding that the safest way to stay inside the machine was to feed it someone else. “Your sister's case,” Ava said slowly, “did it ever connect directly to Krall?” Luca nodded once. “Not cleanly enough to use. But one name kept repeating between her contracts and your client's vendors.” He wrote it on a napkin and turned it toward her. Felix Dane. The same external compliance consultant whose invoice sequence had triggered Ava's first real suspicion in the file. The same man who had charmingly congratulated her at two partner receptions while never once looking directly into her eyes. Cold ran down her back.`,
        `Ava took a photo of the name on the burner phone even though she already knew she would not need help remembering it. The important thing was not the overlap. It was the motive chain. If Felix Dane linked Mara's collapse and Krall's financial labyrinth, then Luca's years of watching had not made him paranoid. They had made him patient. “Why tell me all this now?” she asked. Luca answered without flinching. “Because if your friend really has your phone, you're about to walk into a conversation where half the power is who sounds crazy first. I need you to know you're not chasing shadows.” That should not have been comforting. It was. Ava hated that too. She had not had the luxury of leaning on anyone in years. She was good at being admired for competence and terrible at being helped. Luca seemed built to make that failing inconvenient. When he slid the napkin aside, his fingers brushed hers. Barely anything. Her pulse misbehaved like it had no respect for timing or trauma.`,
        `Sienna's second message arrived while Ava was still angry at her own reaction. Same number. Same white café table. This time the frame was wider, enough to show the reflection in the window behind her. A man stood across the street in mirrored sunglasses, watching the table and not pretending otherwise. On the back of the chair beside Sienna hung Ava's missing handbag. Not just the phone. The bag. Which meant Sienna had been holding more than one piece of evidence the whole time. Below the photo came a new line: If you bring him, I leave. Ava looked up at Luca. He was already reading her face. “She's scared,” he said. Ava shook her head. “No,” she said, staring at the image. “She's being managed.” Then she typed the reply that changed the rest of the day. I'm coming.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Phone Offer",
      paragraphs: [
        `They chose the meeting like people planning a heist and pretending it was coffee. Ava would go alone to the table because Sienna expected that much. Luca would be inside the gallery across the street, where the mirrored front gave him a line of sight without making him obvious. He hated the plan. Ava hated how much she cared that he hated it. They argued in the lane behind the bakery in voices low enough not to attract attention and sharp enough to expose nerves neither of them wanted named. “If she feels cornered, she walks,” Ava said. “If she walks with the phone and the bag, I lose the only direct thread back to last night.” Luca ran a hand through his hair and looked at her like restraint itself was physically taxing. “If she isn't alone, this isn't a meeting,” he said. “It's a handoff.” Ava knew that. She also knew that no one trying to frame her would risk keeping the physical objects forever unless they needed leverage more than disposal. That meant Sienna wanted something. Information, forgiveness, timing. Something negotiable. And negotiations were still Ava's home ground, even if the floor under it had split open.`,
        `They nearly kissed before she left, which was the kind of thing Ava would have mocked mercilessly in anyone else's crisis and could not quite believe in her own. It happened in the half-second after Luca adjusted the collar of the shirt she had not bothered to return and before either of them stepped away. They were too close. He smelled like cedar again, warm skin under pressed cotton, and the look on his face was not hunger exactly. It was conflict. That almost did her in more effectively than seduction would have. Luca's thumb brushed the line of her jaw, as if checking she was real enough to trust with the next hour. Then his phone vibrated and the spell snapped. “If anything feels wrong, you walk,” he said. Ava smiled without warmth. “Everything feels wrong.” A shadow of a smile touched his mouth, gone too fast to study. “Then walk sooner.”`,
        `Sienna had chosen a small café inside a hotel arcade where money disguised itself as discretion. When Ava stepped in, Sienna was already standing. Her relief looked real enough to hurt. So did the tears waiting in her lashes. The bag sat by her chair. The phone lay on the table between them like a truce offering made of explosives. “Ava,” Sienna said, reaching for her. Ava sat instead. “You said alone,” she replied. Sienna's hand stalled in the air, then lowered slowly. “Are you?” she asked, and the question had too many meanings to answer. For a moment neither of them spoke. Ava studied her friend with the hyperclarity that follows betrayal, noticing things she might once have loved: the immaculate nails despite the supposed panic, the slightly raw edge in Sienna's voice that could be genuine or deployed, the way her eyes flicked not just to Ava's face but to the exits. “You lied about last night,” Ava said. Sienna swallowed. “I lied because I was afraid,” she said. “Not of you. Of what happens if certain people think I helped you.”`,
        `That was not a confession. It was an invitation to over-empathize. Ava almost admired the craft. She reached for the phone and Sienna's hand shot out over it faster than either of them could pretend was accidental. There it was: the tiny tell of ownership. Sienna drew back instantly, but the damage was done. “Just listen to me first,” she said. “Please.” Ava folded her hands in her lap and waited. Sienna leaned in, voice trembling at the edges. “You were not supposed to be dragged this far. They only wanted the file and enough pressure to make you back off.” Ava's chest went cold. They. Not Martin. Not someone. They. The architect's pronoun. Sienna realized it a heartbeat too late. Her face emptied. Ava heard the truth click into place with awful, elegant finality. She was not looking at a frightened witness anymore. She was looking at someone who knew the design from inside it.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Wrong Friend",
      paragraphs: [
        `Once Ava saw it, she could not unsee it. Every answer Sienna gave now arrived pre-shaped, not discovered. She knew what had been “meant” to happen. She knew the pressure was supposed to be proportional. She kept speaking about the people behind it as if they were weather, dangerous but impersonal, rather than colleagues with names and appetites. Ava let her talk because panic made careful liars greedy. Sienna said she took the phone because she wanted to keep worse people from getting to it first. She said she took the bag because Ava had been disoriented and leaving it behind would have been stupid. She said she had tried to warn Ava not to push the Krall matter before the partnership vote, but Ava never understood that some wins had prices attached. There it was again. Not concern. Resentment dressed in grief. Sienna was not merely protecting herself. She was justifying a choice she had made long before last night. A choice built from envy and fear and the unbearable practicality of wanting what Ava was about to receive.`,
        `“Who else was on the roof?” Ava asked. Sienna looked down at her coffee. “I don't know everyone's names.” Another mistake. Not because the answer was false, but because it admitted the right category. Ava watched her closely. “Did Martin know?” Sienna flinched almost invisibly and recovered too fast. “Martin knows how this city works,” she said. “That is not the same thing.” Luca had warned her that the person who sounded craziest first would lose. Ava refused the temptation to throw the whole accusation across the table. Instead she leaned back and let her silence do something Sienna was not prepared for: make space. People rushed to fill silence when they were afraid it already contained the truth. “You think I wanted this?” Sienna burst out. “You think I wanted to wake up and realize it had gone that far?” Ava did not answer. Sienna looked almost furious then, not at the accusation but at the lack of absolution. “I thought they would scare you,” she whispered. “I thought you would drop it. I thought maybe you would lose the promotion and still be safe.”`,
        `Safe. The word split something old and tender in Ava cleanly in two. Because in Sienna's mind, apparently, safety meant survivable betrayal. It meant keeping a woman alive while kneecapping her future. It meant ruining a friend rather than challenging a machine. Ava stood so abruptly her chair scraped the marble floor. Heads turned from three nearby tables. Sienna caught her wrist. “Don't leave with nothing,” she hissed, voice breaking open. “The phone is real leverage. So is the file if they didn't copy all of it.” Ava stared at the hand on her wrist until Sienna released it. “You keep saying they,” Ava said quietly. “And every time you do, you sound less scared of them than you are of me understanding you.” Sienna's face blanched. She glanced toward the arcade entrance, and Ava followed the look in time to see mirrored sunglasses disappear behind a column. Not imagined. Not abstract. Supervised.`,
        `Ava took the bag in one clean motion and stepped back before Sienna could stop her. The phone stayed on the table for one impossible second, then Sienna pushed it toward her as if to prove she still controlled the terms. “There are copies on it,” she said quickly. “Not the whole file. Enough. And one message draft you need to read before anyone else does.” Ava picked up the device and slid it into the bag without looking down. Her heart was hammering too hard to trust herself with details here. “If you walk out now,” Sienna said, tears finally spilling free, “you won't understand why I did it.” Ava felt grief rise so violently it almost resembled pity. “No,” she said, voice steady in spite of herself. “If I stay, I might.” Then she turned toward the exit. On her way past the mirrored front she caught Luca's reflection moving toward the door, already reading her face for the answer to a question neither of them wanted.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Memory Room",
      paragraphs: [
        `Luca did not ask if Sienna had confessed. He asked, “What did she admit without meaning to?” That was how Ava knew she could still think beside him even while parts of her were breaking. They took the bag and phone to a storage office above the club that smelled like fresh paint and locked cabinets, and for the first time since waking up, Ava let herself shake. Not daintily. Not with curated dignity. The kind of shaking that started in the spine when your body finally accepted what your mind had already solved. Luca said nothing at first. He simply put a glass of water beside her and stood near enough to make leaving feel optional, not inevitable. When she could speak, she told him exactly where Sienna's story cracked: the use of they, the assumption that the plan's goal was to frighten rather than destroy, the moral logic that called devastation a safety measure. Luca listened, then held out the phone. “Let's see what she thought was worth giving back.”`,
        `The device was partly wiped, but not cleanly. Whoever did the job had removed her messages, banking apps, and the encrypted folder shell, yet missed cached image data and a draft email saved in notes form. More important, one temporary passcode still worked. Sienna had not brought the phone back to negotiate innocence. She had brought it back because she was scared of what it still contained. Ava found a hidden photo burst from the club's roof access corridor and felt the floor tilt under memory again. In one frame she was facing the door, clutch on her shoulder, expression furious. In another, Sienna's hand was at her back, steering. In the third, the man Ava had not recognized was reaching for the bag while Luca moved into frame from the far side. The images unlocked what her mind had been protecting. She had not drifted upstairs to party. She had gone up there to call someone from the board with the file after realizing Martin Howe was about to bury the issue internally. Sienna followed her. So did the man. The rest had become struggle, not decadence.`,
        `They drove back to the club after dark because Ava needed the room itself to pull the final threads free. Luca took her through a private service stair rather than the main entrance, past wine storage and a locked pantry, until they reached the rooftop suite where the shell booking had existed and vanished. The room looked obscene in its normalcy. Velvet banquette. low lights. a mirrored drinks cabinet. The kind of place where consent and performance and secrecy could blur if the wrong people arranged them that way. Ava walked slowly, allowing each object to strike memory where it lived. The overturned coupe glass in the corner. The leather chair angled toward the bed platform. The bathroom door with its gold latch. “I came in here to get away from the corridor,” she murmured. “Sienna said we could use the suite phone because my signal was bad outside.” Luca stayed back, giving her room to arrive at herself. “Then what?” he asked. Ava closed her eyes. “Then she locked the terrace door.”`,
        `Everything after that returned in jagged, merciless pieces. The man from the corridor stepping inside with another one behind him. Sienna telling Ava not to make it uglier than it needed to be. Ava trying to shove the file deeper into her bag and lunge for the suite phone. A glass pressed into her hand she never truly drank from because she was already suspicious. Luca entering before the lock fully caught, saying something sharp enough to break the choreography. The first man smiling as if nothing criminal had happened at all. And Sienna — worst of all, Sienna — turning Ava back toward the room when she tried to bolt for the service stair. Ava opened her eyes and looked at the bed platform with a revulsion so clear it felt cleansing. “I was trying to get out with the file,” she said. Luca's stare sharpened. “And?” Ava swallowed once. “And Sienna handed me back to the people I was trying to escape.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Bedroom Was Staged",
      paragraphs: [
        `Once memory stopped protecting her, details began volunteering themselves with vicious generosity. The torn strap on her dress had not happened in passion; it had happened when she yanked away from the first man's grip. The lipstick-smudged napkin on Luca's side table had not been hers. She did not wear that shade. The snapped chain came from her clutch being pulled open in the suite, not in his apartment. Ava walked through Luca's bedroom now like a prosecutor entering a reconstruction, pointing to everything that had bothered her before she had the words for why. The heel under the chair was placed where someone wanted her eye to find it immediately. The water bottle had been opened with her left hand if the condensation trail meant anything, and Ava was right-handed. The bed itself had barely been slept in on one side. “You didn't wake up here because this happened here,” Luca said quietly. “You woke up here because someone wanted the morning to look finished.” Ava pressed both palms to the dresser until the wood bit back. The setup had not only stolen her credibility. It had borrowed the language of sex to do it. That made it feel intimate in all the worst ways.`,
        `Luca told her what happened after he reached the suite. He had been tracking one of the men for weeks because that man handled private introductions for clients who preferred not to appear connected on paper. When Luca saw him follow Ava upstairs, he moved. Inside the room, he found Ava dizzy but still fighting, Sienna pleading with everyone to calm down in the tone of a person trying to make violence sound managerial. Luca knew better than to start by accusing anyone. He went for the simplest truth in the room: Ava wanted out. He got her into the service lift before the men understood he was no longer asking permission. “You kept saying one thing,” he told her. “Not don't tell anyone. Not call the police. Just one line over and over.” Ava looked up. Luca's face softened for the first time since she had known him. “You said, don't let them turn me into the story.” Something inside her ribcage folded inward. She remembered none of the wording. She remembered the feeling of it so completely she could barely stand.`,
        `The bedroom staging itself, they could now almost map. Sometime after Luca got her to the apartment and left to send one staff member for saline tablets and another to sweep the club's private floor, someone with access to the building briefly entered. Not for long. Long enough to place the props, shift the shoes, and create a morning tableau that would survive one corridor leak and a dozen assumptions. “Which means someone knew where you'd taken me,” Ava said. “Or watched you leave.” Luca nodded. “And someone in my orbit is dirtier than I thought.” That should have made her step back from him again. Instead it made the whole trap look wider, not more personal to him. Multiple layers. The club optics. The firm leak. The email. The phone wipe. The fake aftermath. Sienna had been a lever, not the entire hand. Ava straightened slowly, anger doing what grief could not. “Then Chapter One of their plan was the room,” she said. “Which means Chapter Two was always going to be my career.”`,
        `Luca moved to the desk and set down a slim USB drive. “One of my night managers copied the original service corridor timestamps before someone asked for a silent purge,” he said. “If the room was staged, we can prove the timing gap.” Ava stared at the drive. Real evidence. Not just memory, not just intuition. Evidence that could crack the narrative before it calcified permanently. But the moment hope surfaced, it brought fear with it. If the people behind this had reached into his building systems and her firm's internal channels within hours, then using the proof would not simply defend her. It would declare war. Ava lifted the drive anyway. “Good,” she said, voice steadier than she felt. “Because if they built a bedroom lie to sell a corporate lie, I want both layers in daylight.” Luca's gaze held hers for a beat longer than necessary. “Then daylight is exactly where we take it.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Why Luca Took Her Home",
      paragraphs: [
        `The fight they had been postponing finally arrived after midnight over whiskey neither of them drank. It began with gratitude and turned, the way dangerous truths often did, on motive. Ava had enough of the timeline now to understand the mechanics of her rescue. What she did not understand was Luca's decision before that moment. He had been watching the same network because of Mara. He had followed patterns into his club. He had intervened on the roof because he knew something was wrong. Fine. But that was not the same as innocence. “When you first saw me,” Ava asked, “did you think helping me might lead you to the proof you wanted?” Luca's silence answered before his mouth did. He set the untouched glass down so carefully it made her feel more breakable than shouting would have. “Yes,” he said. “At first.” The honesty was almost cruel in its cleanliness. Ava laughed once, but this time the sound cut upward. Of course. Of course even the man who saved her had begun by seeing utility.`,
        `She stepped away before he could reach for any explanation. All day she had been balancing terror, grief, humiliation, strategic fury, and a chemistry she had no room for. This was the thing that tipped it. Not because his answer made him the villain. Because it made her feel briefly stupid for wanting him to be better than the world they were both navigating. “So I was evidence with nice timing,” she said. Luca's expression sharpened with something that looked a lot like anger at himself. “No,” he said. “You were a woman being cornered. Then you became the only person holding a thread I'd spent years trying to find.” Ava folded her arms over the borrowed shirt she still wore and hated the intimacy of that fact too. “That isn't better.” Luca crossed the room then, slow enough to let her move if she wanted, stopping just beyond the line where touch would have turned the conversation into something else. “It is if you keep going to the end,” he said. “I got you out before I knew whether the file would help me. I kept you hidden after I knew it would.”`,
        `But wounded pride had a logic of its own, and Ava needed distance more than nuance. She took the bag, the burner, the copied timestamps, and the USB drive, and told him she would manage the next steps herself. He let her go, which somehow hurt worse than if he had argued. On the ride back to her apartment, she replayed every minute since waking up and found evidence of his protection everywhere she had least wanted to notice it: the fact he never once implied she owed him trust, the fact he had not used the phone contents to pry into the file before she chose to speak, the fact he had offered information before leverage. By the time she reached her building, anger had already started decaying into something messier. Then the concierge handed her an overnight envelope left under her maiden initials, not her working name. Inside was a printout of building footage from Luca's lobby at dawn. In it, Luca stood between her slumped body and a man in a grey coat, one hand against the apartment doorframe, blocking entry. On the back, in the concierge's neat note, were seven words. This man said nobody touched you.`,
        `Ava sat on the edge of her own bed for nearly an hour after that, staring at the print and hating that vindication could ache this much. He had not only removed her from the suite. He had kept other people from reaching her afterward, even when letting them in might have bought him answers. That did not erase the fact that he had recognized her value. It complicated it beyond easy outrage. And complication, Ava knew too well, was where real feeling usually began. Her burner rang just after one in the morning. Unknown number. She expected Sienna. It was not Sienna. It was Martin Howe, voice smooth as polished stone, asking if they might have a private breakfast before the partnership committee reconvened. “There are narratives here we can still contain,” he said. Ava looked at the still image of Luca bracing his arm against the doorway and felt her pulse go cold again. Martin did not want breakfast. He wanted to see what she still had left.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Alone In The Fallout",
      paragraphs: [
        `Martin chose a private dining room at the Beaumont because men like Martin always believed neutral ground belonged to whoever had been rich the longest. Ava arrived ten minutes early in a slate suit that made her feel like herself again and no one else's myth. She had not messaged Luca. That was partly pride and partly an experiment. If she removed him from the equation, what shape would the pressure take? Martin smiled when she entered with the benevolent sadness of a man rehearsing the role of disappointed mentor. He spoke first about optics, then about timing, then about concern. Only after that careful throat-clearing did he get to the real point: whatever confusion had happened around Krall's matter, it would be best for everyone if Ava took some leave, withdrew from the partnership discussion, and allowed outside counsel to “independently review” the confidentiality breach. Translation: step aside, surrender the promotion, and let the machine digest both scandal and proof without resistance. Ava let him talk until the room smelled of buttered coffee and fraud.`,
        `Then she asked the question she had come to watch him answer. “When did you first learn there were photos?” Martin did not blink. “This morning, like everyone else.” Smooth. Too smooth. Ava slid the printout of the false email across the table instead. “Interesting,” she said. “Because the board received references to my judgment before the photos were public.” That did it. Not a confession, not even a visible flinch. Just a change in the air pressure between them. Martin understood he was no longer performing for a woman still fogged by shame. He leaned back, folded his hands, and for one second stopped pretending paternalism and power were different substances. “Ava,” he said quietly, “high-trust environments do not survive improvised moral heroics.” There it was. Not concern. Doctrine. She smiled then, because rage sometimes wore her best face. “High-trust environments,” she said, “usually don't need fake emails and corridor leaks to discipline one associate.” Martin's eyes cooled. “One associate?” he repeated. “Don't flatter yourself. This is about institutional stability.”`,
        `By the time breakfast ended, Ava knew two things with terrible certainty. First: Martin had expected her to be too shaken to counterpunch intelligently. Second: Sienna had not been freelancing for a promotion alone. Someone inside the firm had accelerated the public leak because the file itself threatened more than one career. She left through the side entrance and found two missed calls from Graham, a voicemail from her mother asking why a gossip page was using cropped club photos, and one message from Sienna that simply said, I'm sorry for the part I played. The wording made Ava stop on the pavement. Part I played. Not mistake. Not misunderstanding. Part. She looked up at the Beaumont's polished windows and understood how much bigger the operation was. Sienna was one hinge. Martin was another. The rest was structure. Without Luca, the scale of it became newly visible and newly brutal. It also made her realize how foolish she had been to think she could untangle all of it alone simply because she was angry.`,
        `When she reached the office, security informed her that access to one client archive had been temporarily restricted pending counsel review. Krall's archive. Before she could answer, her burner buzzed with a text from an unknown encrypted relay. No greeting. No signature. Just a line of plain text that made her mouth go dry. The board packet was timed before the leak. Check internal comms, not client comms. Ava stared at it. That message did not come from Sienna or Martin. It came from someone close enough to the process to know where the choreography began. Someone inside the firm was not merely guilty. Someone inside it had started to wobble.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Corporate Stage",
      paragraphs: [
        `The second layer of the setup was uglier than the first because it required daylight people. Bedrooms could be staged by opportunists and fixers. Partnership votes needed governance. With the relay message burning in her mind, Ava used a dormant admin privilege she had kept from an old crisis migration to pull archived meeting attachments from a server no one thought to lock because no one remembered she had helped structure it. The board packet had indeed been revised before the gossip leak broke. One note in the chair's margin flagged “reputational concerns involving lead associate on Krall matter” six hours before the public photos circulated. Meaning the scandal had not triggered internal caution. Internal caution had prepared a place for the scandal to land. Ava sat very still at her desk as the architecture unfolded. Someone planned not only for her humiliation, but for the precise bureaucratic shelf on which it would be stored. The board. Martin. Outside counsel. The leak. The room. The phone. Every piece was designed to make the next piece feel inevitable.`,
        `She called the one board member she still half trusted, Eleanor Pike, and asked no direct questions. Instead she presented a hypothetical: if a reputational concern were planted to influence a partnership decision, how early would it need to be seeded to survive counsel review? Eleanor, who had been underestimated for twenty years by men whose names ended up beneath hers, answered in the dry cadence of someone pretending they were discussing weather. “Early enough that the review appears reactive when it is actually rehearsed.” Then, after a pause, “Ava, if someone is using governance as theater, do not confuse the first act with the opening night.” Ava closed her eyes. Chapter thirteen had exposed the intimate staging. This was the corporate version. Not lust, but liability. Not shame, but governance. The two layers mirrored each other too perfectly to be coincidence. Someone had built a sex-adjacent scandal because institutions still found that story easier to believe than fraud with polished signatures.`,
        `Luca appeared at her office door that evening without warning and without apology, which was almost enough to make her laugh. He took one look at the wall of printed timelines, margin notes, and vendor cross-links she had turned her office into and said, “So much for handling it alone.” Ava should have bristled. Instead she handed him the annotated board packet. He read in silence, then looked up with something like respect and something darker braided together. “This is better,” he said. “The room proves narrative manipulation. This proves premeditation.” Ava nodded, suddenly too tired to pretend she had not missed him. “And it proves Sienna wasn't the architect.” Luca placed the packet down. “No,” he said. “It proves she was recruited by people who knew how to turn one betrayal into policy.” He then laid something on her desk that pulled the air from the room. An attendance log from a private charity gala the month before. Martin Howe. Felix Dane. Krall. Sienna. And one name Ava had not expected at all: Graham Mercer.`,
        `Her ex was not the mastermind. She knew that instantly. Graham liked comfort more than risk. But he loved access, and he loved being adjacent to power if it made him feel chosen. If he had been in the same room as Dane and Sienna, then the scheme had braided personal vanity into professional sabotage with nauseating efficiency. Ava sat back in her chair, staring at the page until the names almost detached from language. Luca watched her carefully. “You said the problem was bigger than Sienna,” he said. “This is bigger.” Ava nodded once, because speech had temporarily left her. Then she thought of the one thing they still lacked. “We can prove structure now,” she said at last. “But we still can't prove the final hour. The part where I tried to protect the file instead of destroy myself.” Luca reached into his jacket and pulled out a slim memory card. “Then maybe it's time,” he said, “to stop looking at their paperwork and go back to my cameras.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "Back To Luca",
      paragraphs: [
        `Ava went back to Luca's apartment because she chose to, which changed the room entirely. It was still spare, still dark-lined, still built by someone who trusted function more than ease. But choice rearranged the air. So did the fact that this time she arrived holding evidence instead of waking inside it. Luca made pasta at one in the morning because apparently the man who could read criminal choreography from a corridor camera also believed hunger made people reckless. They ate standing at the kitchen counter over a laptop running security archives, pausing footage and comparing timestamps until the hours thinned into something private. For the first time since the scandal began, Ava felt like part of a team rather than a target in motion. They argued cleanly, folded their conclusions together, called each other on leaps, corrected each other's bias. It should have been purely practical. Instead it carried the dangerous intimacy of being understood quickly by someone equally sharp and equally damaged.`,
        `The missing piece appeared in fragments, not revelation. A service-elevator clip showed Sienna entering alone at 1:12 a.m. and exiting at 1:46 with Ava's bag. Another angle caught Felix Dane speaking briefly with Graham in the club's covered arrival lane at 12:58. Most important, one muted hallway feed from outside a staff-only telecom closet showed a junior IT contractor Ava recognized from Vale & Howe's vendor rotation entering the club's back systems room at 2:03 a.m. That was the bridge. The club leak and the firm leak were not parallel improvisations. They shared an operations hand. “If we identify him fully, we connect the video purge and the email compromise,” Ava said. Luca nodded, but his gaze stayed on her a beat too long. “And if we do that, you stop being a rumor problem and start being a live threat.” She looked up. “I already am.” Luca's mouth shifted with the ghost of humor she had begun to crave. “Yes,” he said. “But right now only to people smart enough to notice.”`,
        `Sometime near three, exhaustion finally cracked Ava's performance enough that she stopped being careful with silence. “I did think you used me,” she said, not looking at him. “At least for a while.” Luca closed the laptop lid halfway and leaned on the counter. “I know.” “And I hated that you might still be the most decent man in the room.” That drew a real, startled laugh out of him, low and brief and warmer than anything she had heard since they met. The sound did something reckless to her chest. “That isn't a sentence I've been given often,” he said. Ava turned then, intending to say something clever and finding nothing useful at all. Luca stepped toward her slowly, leaving a last clean inch of choice between them. “You don't owe me trust because I did the right thing late,” he said quietly. “But I am asking for it now.” The honesty of that nearly undid her. So she kissed him before she could decide whether it was strategic folly, grief response, or the most alive thing she had felt all week.`,
        `It was not a careless kiss. It was a pause made physical. His hand settled lightly at the back of her neck, not owning, just anchoring. When she pulled back, both of them looked a little stunned and a little angry at how inevitable it had felt. Then the laptop on the counter chimed with a file recovery notice, because apparently even desire in Ava's life had adopted catastrophic timing. The restored fragment was not the whole archive and not the final hour. It was better. A cached club audio file from the roof service corridor, clipped and damaged but legible enough to make both of them go still. Ava heard Sienna's voice first. Then her own. Then Felix Dane saying the line that finally turned suspicion into a prosecutable shape: “Once the board packet moves, nobody will care whether she was telling the truth.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Public Set-Up",
      paragraphs: [
        `The charity foundation gala at the Lonsdale Hotel had been designed for three things: polished money, discreet alliances, and selective amnesia. Which made it perfect. Martin Howe would be there, because men like Martin always attended events where reputations wore black tie. Krall would be there because public generosity made private fraud look almost responsible. Felix Dane would be there because consultants were hardest to question when they hovered outside official charts. Ava should have been absent, disgraced, and quietly negotiating a leave announcement by then. Instead she arrived in a dark satin gown that made every whisper in the entrance line sharpen into intent. Luca walked half a pace behind and to her left, not claiming her and not allowing anyone else to do so. By the time they reached the ballroom, the story had already shifted. Not cleared. Interested. A ruined woman hid. A dangerous one entered smiling. Ava could work with interest.`,
        `The plan was not theatrical, though it would later be described that way by people who mistook confidence for drama. Eleanor Pike had agreed to draw three board members into a side salon under the pretext of urgent governance exposure. Luca's recovered audio would be played only after Ava established the timeline gap between the board packet and the public leak. Martin would then have to explain why reputational concerns were seeded before the scandal became public and why an outside consultant tied to the same client had been on Luca's private floor that night. It was not perfect. They still lacked the emotional proof that would clear Ava completely in the eyes of people who preferred scandal to spreadsheets. But it was enough to break the institutional choreography. “Once this starts, it won't stay contained,” Luca warned quietly as the string quartet shifted into a slower piece. Ava took the champagne flute a server offered her, held it without drinking, and answered with the calm she had rebuilt from scratch. “Neither did their version.”`,
        `The first rupture came faster than expected. Sienna saw Ava across the ballroom and visibly lost the rhythm of her smile. Graham, beside her, looked like a man discovering too late that a private mistake had become a public category. Martin recovered fastest, of course. He crossed the room with the warm concern of a statesman approaching a national embarrassment. “Ava,” he said. “I wasn't sure you'd feel up to this.” Ava tilted her head. “That must be difficult for you,” she replied, “since so many of your assumptions this week have turned out to be premature.” It was a small line, but it landed. She could feel it in the circle of attention widening around them. Martin's expression did not crack, though his eyes cooled by a degree. Luca said nothing. He simply stood there with the kind of controlled menace polite men hated because it refused to flatter their status. When Eleanor touched Ava's elbow and said the salon was ready, Martin finally looked uncertain.`,
        `Inside the side room, the technical proof did exactly what it was supposed to do. The board packet revision appeared on screen. The timestamp predated the public leak. The corridor audio clip put Felix Dane in the chain. The service-elevator stills put Sienna with Ava's bag and phone. Martin tried governance language. Dane tried indignation. Sienna tried tears. Then Graham, already wilting at the edges, made the mistake weak men always make under heat: he blamed the wrong person too specifically. “This wasn't supposed to become a criminal thing,” he snapped at Sienna. The room went silent. Ava felt the first crack of actual fear move through the others. Good. Let it. But as the evidence tightened, one fact remained maddeningly incomplete. It proved the machine. It did not yet fully restore the woman crushed beneath it. Technical proof could dismantle policy. Emotional proof was what would rescue her name. And somewhere, still missing, was the final hour that explained who she had been when the trap was closing.`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Voice Memo",
      paragraphs: [
        `The emotional proof arrived from the one place Ava had stopped looking because it felt too small to matter. Her old smartwatch. The one she had stopped wearing halfway through the night because the band had irritated her wrist and she remembered tossing it into the lining pocket of her coat. The coat had been recovered with her apartment dry cleaning that afternoon, overlooked because it seemed irrelevant next to phones and files and betrayal. But the watch had voice-trigger emergency capture enabled, a setting Ava activated months ago after one too many late-night client blowups and never thought about again. When Luca got the battery coaxed alive enough to sync, the file list populated with one corrupted clip time-stamped 1:41 a.m. Twenty-two minutes of broken audio, mostly static, rooftop wind, footsteps, pieces of voices cutting in and out. Ava sat on Luca's floor with the laptop between them while the waveform loaded like a held breath. “If this is nothing, it's still nothing honest,” Luca said. She almost laughed. Then the file opened, and the past walked back into the room.`,
        `The first clear thing they heard was Ava's own voice, sharper than she remembered ever sounding. “No,” she said into the wind and interference. “It doesn't go to Martin.” Sienna answered too quickly. “Ava, please, be smart.” Then a male voice — Felix, unmistakable once they knew him — said, “Nobody is accusing anyone. We just need to make sure the material doesn't go somewhere regrettable.” Ava closed her eyes. She could hear herself moving, the scrape of shoes, the lift in her breathing as adrenaline took over. “You're not getting my phone,” she said. “Or the file.” Then a new sound entered, one she had not consciously remembered and that hit harder because of it: Luca's voice, colder than she had ever heard it. “Step away from her.” The silence that followed was the charged kind produced when predators realize another predator has entered the room and may not be aligned. Static swallowed several seconds. Then came Sienna, low and urgent, saying the line that finished her: “If she keeps this, we're all done.”`,
        `What followed was messy, human, undeniable. Not scandal. Resistance. Ava hearing herself say, “Don't let them turn me into the story,” not as a metaphor but as a plea flung at the only person in reach who had not yet sold her. Luca answering without hesitation, “Then come with me.” The next burst carried shuffling, a door impact, someone cursing, Sienna shouting Ava's name with fear that sounded partly real because perhaps it was. Betrayal did not always erase feeling. Sometimes it made it grotesque. Near the end of the clip came the proof Ava did not know she needed until she heard it. Half-conscious, furious, still lucid enough to cling to purpose, she said, “The folder isn't in the phone. It never was.” Luca's reply came immediately, almost protective in its bluntness. “Good,” he said. “Then they don't get either of you tonight.” Ava pressed a hand to her mouth. There it was. Her courage, when no one expected performance. His protection, before trust, before leverage, before any clean reason to keep going.`,
        `They played the clip in the same side salon where Martin's governance script had begun to fail. This time the room did not merely cool. It recoiled. The audio did what spreadsheets could not. It restored sequence, agency, and moral gravity. Ava had not stumbled drunkenly into ruin. She had been holding a line. Luca had not hidden a scandalous woman. He had removed a targeted one from a trap. Sienna's tears changed flavor at once, from persuading to useless. Graham looked ill. Felix Dane asked for counsel and lost the right to sound sophisticated while doing it. And Martin Howe, listening to Ava's own voice refuse him in real time, finally looked like a man seeing his era end in a language he could not interrupt. Luca turned to her after the clip stopped, the room still frozen around them. Quietly, just for her, he said the line the recording had saved for the right moment. “You asked me not to let them turn you into the story,” he said. “And I promised you I wouldn't.”`
      ]
    },
    {
      title: "The Real Story",
      paragraphs: [
        `The collapse, when it came, was less dramatic than movies lied about and more satisfying than Ava had allowed herself to imagine. It sounded like lawyers changing tone. It looked like board members stopping Martin mid-sentence instead of nodding him through one more graceful distortion. Felix Dane's devices were seized before midnight under a warrant triggered by documentation Ava never got to see and no longer needed to. Graham, cornered by the possibility of becoming useful only as a minor conspirator, volunteered more than anyone expected about dinners, introductions, and the way Martin liked young professionals with ambition because ambition made leverage cheaper. Sienna did not confess beautifully. Ava had never really expected that. She confessed in fragments, with self-justifying honesty, the kind that hurt because it still wanted sympathy. She had told herself Ava would recover. She had told herself the promotion would have changed their friendship anyway. She had told herself survival sometimes meant choosing who got pushed overboard before the ship listed. None of it helped. None of it reached the part of Ava that had once loved her enough to mistake proximity for loyalty.`,
        `The partnership vote was postponed, then restructured, then dragged into public daylight in a way Vale & Howe had spent a century avoiding. Ava expected to feel triumphant when Martin's resignation became unavoidable. Instead she felt tired in the oldest places in her body. Institutions did not heal because one elegant fraud ring was exposed. They adapted and called it lessons learned. What mattered more was smaller and harder won. Her name stopped being a scandal and became what it should have been from the start: the name of the person who found the truth, resisted the coercion, and survived the machine built to discredit her. Eleanor Pike told her, dry as ever, that the firm would now have to decide whether it still deserved ambitious women at all. Krall's board retained outside investigators and quietly severed half the vendor web before the papers could make it public. Mara received formal acknowledgment at last that her case had been mishandled and maliciously framed. It was not enough to restore the years taken from her. It was enough to return certainty where gaslighting had lived.`,
        `Luca gave Ava space for exactly forty-eight hours after the gala, which she privately thought was smug because he knew the number would haunt her and then soften. On the third evening she found him on the club's river terrace just after sunset, tie undone, sleeves rolled, looking less like a myth and more like a man who had been carrying too much weight in private for too long. Neither of them opened with apologies. There had been too much truth between them for performance to survive. Ava told him the board had offered her the partnership again, privately, packaged as a correction. She had said no. Not because she was weak. Because taking back the exact future they had nearly buried her under no longer felt like victory. She wanted terms built by choice, not repaired optics. Luca looked at her for a long moment and smiled in that rare, dangerous way that made his whole face younger and more tired at once. “That sounds expensive,” he said. Ava stepped closer. “I bill accordingly.”`,
        `When she kissed him this time, there was no panic in it, no interruption, no need to prove who had protected whom first. Just decision. The river below them flashed gold under the city lights. Somewhere behind them, a hundred private conversations kept trying to rearrange the world into something convenient. Ava no longer cared. The night that had been designed to destroy her now belonged to a different story — not because scandal had turned romantic, but because sabotage had failed to erase the truth of who she was when pressure closed in. She had not become the cautionary headline. She had become the witness who survived it, the strategist who refused the script, the woman who discovered that the one stranger in the room had been the first person to protect her without asking what she was worth in return. Luca touched his forehead to hers and said, almost like a vow, “No one gets to use your ruin again.” Ava smiled, held his gaze, and answered with the truth she had earned. “Good,” she said. “Because this time, I get to choose the story.”`
      ]
    }
  ]
} as const;

export default scandalArticle;
